$h(x)=\underbrace{(~\overbrace{5-6x}^{\text{inner}}~)^5}_{\text{outer}}$

So if $h(x)=w(u(x))$, then:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{u(x)}&=\goldD{5-6x} &&\text{inner function}
\\\\
w(x)&=x^5&&\text{outer function}
\end{align}$

Before applying the chain rule, let's find the derivatives of the inner and outer functions (work not shown, but hopefully these derivatives are familiar by now).

$\begin{align}
\maroonD{u'(x)}&=\maroonD{-6}
\\\\
\blueD{w'(x)}&=\blueD{5x^4}
\end{align}$

Now let's apply the chain rule:

$\begin{align}
h'(x)& =\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[w\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]
\\\\
&=\blueD{w'\Bigl(\goldD{u(x)}\Bigr)}\cdot\maroonD{u'(x)}
\\\\
&=\blueD{5(\goldD{5-6x})^4} \cdot \maroonD{-6}
\\\\
&=-30(5-6x)^4
\end{align}$
